From 54d4f1668eabacd499a757a28079c9669e016848 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 20:35:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] FEX: cap frame time from FEX; time dispatches and copy
 compute shaders

Cap pre-sleep: the cap's period was the median of the intervals between
presents the cap held 3 ms or more. Frames slower than the cap get long
holds from the window system too, at the frame time, so while loading
in the median rose (fex21 log: 25.0 -> 30.4 ms at a 25 ms cap). With a
period that long every pre-slept present reached the cap after its due
time, the cap never held one long again, nothing corrected it, and the
game ran at 37 FPS at a 40 FPS cap for the whole session (also seen at
30 FPS caps). Now FEX writes the cap's own frame time (fps_limit rounded
to whole refreshes of GameNative's vsync.txt period, as the cap layer
does) into the new export vkd3d_fex_cap_grid, and the pre-sleep uses it
unless the learned median is more than 1 ms shorter. Without FEX's
figure the learned period is the lower quartile, and it is dropped when
pre-sleeping goes 90 presents without a long hold. The cap line says
where the period came from.

Pass meter, measured frames: each Dispatch is timed alone (all-commands
timestamps right before and after it) and added up per compute shader
('gpu dispatches' line, costliest 6). The game's work between render
passes cost 2.3-3.4 ms a frame in the fex21 log, mostly in gaps with
dispatches. While the meter runs the shader copy takes every compute
shader's D3D bytecode and SPIR-V once (kinds 10/11, up to 64 shaders,
VKD3D_FEX_COPY_COMPUTE=0 turns it off); a pipeline made from the
driver's cache has no SPIR-V at hand and is compiled again for the
copy. Shader copy table 128 -> 512 entries.

VKD3D_FEX_ROOT_DEDUPE is on by default now (0 off, 2 by turns every
second): the fex21 log showed the same GPU and CPU time per draw either
way, with fewer push descriptor sets and constants sent.
